As shown in the diagram, let $NT = a$ and $NS = b$.  Since the radius is half the diameter,  we have \begin{align*}
a^2 + 18^2 &= 25^2 \\
a^2 &= 475 \text{ (1)}
\end{align*}  and \begin{align*}
b^2 + 12^2 &= 25^2 \\
b^2 &= 509 \text{ (2)}
\end{align*} The square of the length for segment $PQ$ can be found by summing the squares of sides in right triangle $PQZ$ minus twice the area of the triangle.  Thus, \begin{align*}
PQ^2 &= (a + b)^2 + 25^2 - 2\cdot (a + b)\cdot 25\cdot \sin 42^\circ \\
&= a^2 + b^2 + 2ab + 25^2 - 2ab - 2ab\cdot \sin 42^\circ \\
&= 1050 + 2ab\cdot (1 - \sin 42^\circ) \\
&= 1036 + 2\cdot \sqrt{475\cdot 509}(1 - \sin 42^\circ)
\end{align*}

